  "surprise": "Discard: 638 (639, 652, 622) against 704's 676 (682, 680, 667), below on every seed. The feedback is not what the model learned to use. Pretraining val loss at 8 thoughts minus at 0: -0.0014, +0.0008, -0.0013 (predicted at least -0.002 on every seed; 704: -0.0010, -0.0002, +0.0000), so thoughts fed the plain network's state at the trigger reduce the loss no more than 704's pause slots; the prediction failed on every seed, and a seed went the wrong way. The gain opened from 0 to a mean |weight| of 0.042/0.041/0.044 after pretraining (predicted above 0.2) and RL left it there (0.043/0.042/0.045). Scale: a LayerNorm output has unit variance per element, so at 0.04 the fed-back vector has the per-element scale of a token embedding at initialization (std 0.02), not nothing; whether it is small against the trained embeddings and slot vectors is unmeasured (checkpoints are not pulled). Either way the loss did not pull it further: with the draft detached, the thoughts find nothing in the model's own reading of the context that they could not already reach through attention. 701-703's used thoughts had gradient through the fed-back state, and that joint training remains the surviving explanation. The score is the cost of a second network with no benefit. Pretraining got 2,937-2,974 steps (704: 3,398-3,784) at the same end val loss at 8 thoughts (0.0832-0.0858 against 704's 0.0843-0.0869), so per step the feedback model learns a little more, as the bench said. In RL every token runs through both networks: sampling 119-153 samples/s (704: 181-207), rounds of 350-493 s (704: 267-329), so 4 rounds fit instead of 6-7, 1,097-1,125 RL targets (704: 1,173-1,191), 1,511-1,644 s charged, total 2,932-3,266 s. metric_diag 571/625/572, 27-68 below metric (per-sample eval accuracy 0.40-0.41 at 8 thoughts against 0.30-0.36 at 0; 704: 0.43-0.45 at 8), where 704's seeds 0 and 1 sat 12-24 below: with the val loss flat in n this is the no-thought path drifting under the 8-only fine-tune (perhaps a little further here because at 8 the model also sees the 0.04-scaled draft), not computation in the thoughts. Round-1 acceptance 4.2/6.0/6.8% at 8 thoughts, held-out greedy 0.936-0.958: the weak kind of pretrained model again (704: 18.9/11.4/5.4; 706: 5.3/4.8/2.8). Next: the zero gain init may be a trap (the thoughts' downstream weights train on the slots, a random-direction draft gets a small gradient on the gain, and nothing forces it open); gain 1 from the start, 701-703's init, is untested here and costs one run. The other reading, that the gradient through the fed-back state is what makes thoughts matter, needs a second backward (702's 0.45x speed) and the speed problem solved first.",
